  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to minimize the maximum shift overlap of a step function h:[0,2] -> [0,1] with integral 1. The current method uses expensive local optimization with softmax approximations. This new approach leverages the known symmetry of the optimal solution and a smoother surrogate to reduce computational cost. The key limitations are the high dimensionality and non-convexity, but our parametric fitting allows us to capture the likely structure of the solution efficiently.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n1. Discrete representation: h is defined on n_points samples spaced dx=2/n apart\n2. Objective: C5 = max_{k} (compute_k_overlap(h, k)) * dx\n3. Constraint: \u2211h_i * dx = 1 with h_i \u2208 [0,1]\n4. Symmetry assumption: Optimal solution is symmetric about the center of [0,2]\n5. Parametric form: Fit a piecewise linear symmetric profile to minimize the objective\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Precompute shifted correlations for efficiency\n2. Use symmetry to halve optimization dimensions\n3. Fit piecewise linear profile to the optimal x-axis\n4. Global optimization via Bayesian sampling and local refinement\n\nSearch Choices:\n1. Vary grid resolution from 100 to 400 points (ensuring even numbers)\n2. Sweep alpha from 5 to 30 for the smoother objective\n3. Use random starts + grid-upgrades + parent-to-child transfer\n4. Maintain dimensionality by mirroring across center\n\nBudget and Robustness:\nTime estimate: 1100 seconds for 3-5 optimization runs at 400 points\n1. Check elapsed time before expensive solver calls\n2. Time-based termination for inner iterations\n3. Fallback to parent solution if no improvement\n\nValidation:\n1. Ensure symmetry post-optimization: make_symmetric(h)\n2. Verify integral: np.sum(h) * dx (should equal 1)\n3. Re-verify C5 using full correlation\n\nReturn Format:\n(h_values, c5_bound, n_points) where h_values enforces [0,1] with sum=np.sum(h_values)*dx==1",
